WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Let's begin with a simple fact.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.160
People make inconsistent decisions.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.120
That's the way it is.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.800
What was initially mere intuition has since been validated

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:13.980
by over 40 years' academic research

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.540
on the decision-making process.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:21.540
The name of this revolutionary theory is behavioral economics,

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.520
whose major message,

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.880
as supported by Nobel Prize Award winner Daniel Kahneman,

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.440
overthrows the paradigm of traditional economics.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.200
Hello, I'm behavioral economics.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.880
And I'm standard economics.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.160
Isn't this a wonderful world that we live in?

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.180
People are always making rational decisions all the time,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.400
not letting their emotions cloud their judgment

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.380
and always thinking about the future.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.720
I just don't see how you can say this.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.360
We see stock bubbles.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.400
We see people overextending themselves on credit cards.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.520
We see people taking mortgages they shouldn't be taking.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.760
I just don't see where this rationality is coming from.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.240
We are not rational beings.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.120
Rather, we are influenced by cognitive bias

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:06.040
and mental shortcuts, by our emotions, and by other factors,

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.300
as well as by the environment and setting where we are

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.120
at the point a decision is made.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:12.600
Why is it so important for marketers?

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.280
Because if you miss the learnings from behavioral economics

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.880
on what are the real drivers which influence consumers,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.680
you may design marketing actions which are not efficient.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.760
Building upon this proven approach of observing behavior,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.080
we developed Nudge.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.280
Through better understanding of human behavior

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.360
in both its rational and irrational dimensions,

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.000
it becomes possible to take more effective action

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.840
to modify behavior as we wish.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.000
The idea of Nudge was developed by Professor Cass Sunstein

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:48.000
and Richard Thaler in 2008 in their book entitled Nudge.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.560
Nudge is a system of gentle encouragements

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.200
based on advanced knowledge of the decision-making process.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:55.640
The benefits of Nudge?

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:58.040
Remarkable power to change behaviors

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.200
through cost-effective actions.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.480
Examples?

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.240
How can we encourage households

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.600
to reduce their energy consumption?

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:07.880
Simply by amending a letter informing them

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.320
about their energy use,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.600
adding a comparison with their neighbor's energy use.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.840
If your energy use is lower, you are congratulated.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:16.920
It's great!

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.760
With two smiley.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.160
If this is not the case,

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.560
you are sent hints and tips to easily improve.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.960
By using social norm drivers,

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:30.000
the O-Power team has saved some $250 million

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.600
and all without spending a dollar.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.520
So Nudge can be used to save the planet.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.520
Another example, this time from the UK healthcare sector.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.440
How to encourage people to take part

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.560
in the National Organ Donor Program

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.720
through the addition of a simple phrase

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.600
on the government's official website.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.160
The phrase in question?

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.920
Each day, thousands of people who see this page

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.680
decide to register.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.920
Once more, social standards pressure worked its magic

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:58.240
with the test showing potential

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.240
for 96,000 additional registrations

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.240
and once more at zero cost.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.360
And this is what Nudge is all about.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.800
A simple, effective, and low-cost technique

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.920
to encourage the adoption of new behaviors.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.480
As we have seen in our various examples,

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.080
local authorities have been quick

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.880
to grasp the potential of Nudge.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.840
First was the Obama administration in 2009,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.440
just before the Cameron government in 2010,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.560
followed by Australia, New Zealand,

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.800
and very recently Germany and the European Commission.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.600
Since then, the Nudge approach has developed very quickly,

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.920
powered by the attraction of its effectiveness

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.880
in modifying behaviors and its low-cost implementation.

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.360
Private companies are also beginning to discover Nudge,

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.040
which appears to be a clear way to improve ROI

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:46.760
from their marketing strategies

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.760
as illustrated by this small change

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.040
which has doubled the sales

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.480
from a promotional Campbell Soup offer.

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.160
How to make this happen without any additional cost?

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.080
By communicating what Nudgers call an anchor to consumers

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:01.360
at the moment of the decision

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.440
with this sentence written on the promotional sign,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.280
limit of 12 cans per person.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.480
Results, with this anchor,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.800
sales goes from 3.3 to seven cans per buyer.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.440
It has activated the idea of a good deal

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.360
more than the promotional offer alone.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.480
As proof of this growing interest,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.160
the largest global trade fair for market research

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.360
has seen successive presentations

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.640
by the founder of Nudge, Richard Thaler,

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.080
by Nobel Prize winner, Daniel Kahneman,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.960
and finally by the famous Duke University professor

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.240
and author of the bestseller,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.880
predictably irrational, Dan Ariely.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.960
In the September 2015 edition

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.800
of the Harvard Business Review,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.600
Indra Nooyi, global CEO of PepsiCo, stated,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.920
we've taken lessons from Richard Thaler

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.920
and Cass Sustine's book, Nudge.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.760
The Nudge revolution is in progress.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.520
Since the establishment of its first shopper lab

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.440
some 25 years ago,

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.200
In Vivo BVA has always sought inspiration

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.880
from behavioral economics

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:03.920
with a view to developing

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:06.400
increasingly predictive study methods,

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.760
which can provide results through studies, testing,

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.960
and analysis of consumer behaviors

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.440
undertaken in near real-life conditions.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.320
We were the first to create a Nudge unit

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.280
in the world of market research,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:21.840
specializing in the development

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.320
of behavioral change strategies.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:27.960
The Nudge Lab, our exclusive and innovative methodology,

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.880
rooted in behavioral economics

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.040
and inspired by creativity techniques,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.040
design thinking, and consultancy,

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.640
allows the development of powerful nudges

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.880
based on rigorous production processes.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.440
This work has been crowned with success,

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.200
with Nudge Lab receiving the award

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.040
for excellence for best paper

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.280
at the SOMAR World Congress in 2015.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.840
On the back of our successes,

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.160
we have since published the very first work

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.240
on Nudge marketing,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.240
dedicated to the application of Nudge in the public

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.240
as well as the private sector.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:02.840
We invite you too to share in the Nudge adventure

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.280
by coming to discover this revolutionary approach

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.720
for yourself.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.280
Our goal, it is to help you benefit

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.280
from the power of the Nudge approach

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.760
to win at behavioral change.
